root[146]
├─0   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 1
│     └─0 text "The Ruff Formatter"
├─1   paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "The Ruff formatter is an extremely fast Python code formatter designed as a drop-in replacement for\n"
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://pypi.org/project/black/"
│     │   └─0 text "Black"
│     ├─2 text ", available as part of the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "ruff"
│     ├─4 text " CLI via "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "ruff format"
│     └─6 text "."
├─2   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 inlineCode "ruff format"
├─3   paragraph[2]
│     ├─0 inlineCode "ruff format"
│     └─1 text " is the primary entrypoint to the formatter. It accepts a list of files or\ndirectories, and formats all discovered Python files:"
├─4   code "ruff format                   # Format all files in the current directory.\nruff format path/to/code/     # Format all files in `path/to/code` (and any subdirectories).\nruff format path/to/file.py   # Format a single file."
│       lang: "shell"
│       meta: null
├─5   paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Similar to Black, running "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "ruff format /path/to/file.py"
│     ├─2 text " will format the given file or directory\nin-place, while "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "ruff format --check /path/to/file.py"
│     └─4 text " will avoid writing any formatted files back,\nand instead exit with a non-zero status code upon detecting any unformatted files."
├─6   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "For the full list of supported options, run "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "ruff format --help"
│     └─2 text "."
├─7   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Philosophy"
├─8   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "The initial goal of the Ruff formatter is "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "not"
│     └─2 text " to innovate on code style, but rather, to innovate\non performance, and provide a unified toolchain across Ruff's linter, formatter, and any and all\nfuture tools."
├─9   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "As such, the formatter is designed as a drop-in replacement for "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/psf/black"
│     │   └─0 text "Black"
│     └─2 text ",\nbut with an excessive focus on performance and direct integration with Ruff. Given Black's\npopularity within the Python ecosystem, targeting Black compatibility ensures that formatter\nadoption is minimally disruptive for the vast majority of projects."
├─10  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Specifically, the formatter is intended to emit near-identical output when run over existing\nBlack-formatted code. When run over extensive Black-formatted projects like Django and Zulip, > 99.9%\nof lines are formatted identically. (See: "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "#style-guide"
│     │   └─0 emphasis[1]
│     │       └─0 text "Style Guide"
│     └─2 text ".)"
├─11  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Given this focus on Black compatibility, the formatter thus adheres to "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://black.readthedocs.io/en/stable/the_black_code_style/current_style.html"
│     │   └─0 text "Black's (stable) code style"
│     └─2 text ",\nwhich aims for \"consistency, generality, readability and reducing git diffs\". To give you a sense\nfor the enforced code style, here's an example:"
├─12  code "# Input\ndef _make_ssl_transport(\n    rawsock, protocol, sslcontext, waiter=None,\n    *, server_side=False, server_hostname=None,\n    extra=None, server=None,\n    ssl_handshake_timeout=None,\n    call_connection_made=True):\n    '''Make an SSL transport.'''\n    if waiter is None:\n      waiter = Future(loop=loop)\n\n    if extra is None:\n      extra = {}\n\n    ...\n\n# Ruff\ndef _make_ssl_transport(\n    rawsock,\n    protocol,\n    sslcontext,\n    waiter=None,\n    *,\n    server_side=False,\n    server_hostname=None,\n    extra=None,\n    server=None,\n    ssl_handshake_timeout=None,\n    call_connection_made=True,\n):\n    \"\"\"Make an SSL transport.\"\"\"\n    if waiter is None:\n        waiter = Future(loop=loop)\n\n    if extra is None:\n        extra = {}\n\n    ..."
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─13  paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "Like Black, the Ruff formatter does "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "not"
│     ├─2 text " support extensive code style configuration; however,\nunlike Black, it "
│     ├─3 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "does"
│     ├─4 text " support configuring the desired quote style, indent style, line endings,\nand more. (See: "
│     ├─5 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "#configuration"
│     │   └─0 emphasis[1]
│     │       └─0 text "Configuration"
│     └─6 text ".)"
├─14  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "While the formatter is designed to be a drop-in replacement for Black, it is not intended to be\nused interchangeably with Black on an ongoing basis, as the formatter "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "does"
│     ├─2 text " differ from\nBlack in a few conscious ways (see: "
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "formatter/black.md"
│     │   └─0 emphasis[1]
│     │       └─0 text "Known deviations"
│     └─4 text "). In general,\ndeviations are limited to cases in which Ruff's behavior was deemed more consistent, or\nsignificantly simpler to support (with negligible end-user impact) given the differences in the\nunderlying implementations between Black and Ruff."
├─15  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Going forward, the Ruff Formatter will support Black's preview style under Ruff's own\n"
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "preview.md"
│     │   └─0 text "preview"
│     └─2 text " mode."
├─16  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Configuration"
├─17  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "The Ruff Formatter exposes a small set of configuration options, some of which are also supported\nby Black (like line width), some of which are unique to Ruff (like quote, indentation style and\nformatting code examples in docstrings)."
├─18  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, to configure the formatter to use single quotes, format code\nexamples in docstrings, a line width of 100, and tab indentation, add the\nfollowing to your configuration file:"
├─19  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─20  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff]\nline-length = 100\n\n[tool.ruff.format]\nquote-style = \"single\"\nindent-style = \"tab\"\ndocstring-code-format = true\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─21  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─22  code "```toml\nline-length = 100\n\n[format]\nquote-style = \"single\"\nindent-style = \"tab\"\ndocstring-code-format = true\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
