├─28  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "If a code example is recognized and treated as Python, the Ruff formatter will\nautomatically skip it if the code does not parse as valid Python or if the\nreformatted code would produce an invalid Python program."
├─29  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Users may also configure the line length limit used for reformatting Python\ncode examples in docstrings. The default is a special value, "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "dynamic"
│     ├─2 text ", which\ninstructs the formatter to respect the line length limit setting for the\nsurrounding Python code. The "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "dynamic"
│     └─4 text " setting ensures that even when code\nexamples are found inside indented docstrings, the line length limit configured\nfor the surrounding Python code will not be exceeded. Users may also configure\na fixed line length limit for code examples in docstrings."
├─30  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, this configuration shows how to enable docstring code formatting\nwith a fixed line length limit:"
├─31  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─32  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff.format]\ndocstring-code-format = true\ndocstring-code-line-length = 20\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
