│     └─0 text "Fluent layout for method chains"
├─134 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "At times, when developers write long chains of methods on an object, such as"
├─135 code "x = df.filter(cond).agg(func).merge(other)"
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─136 paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "the intent is to perform a sequence of transformations or operations\non a fixed object of interest - in this example, the object "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "df"
│     ├─2 text ".\nAssuming the assigned expression exceeds the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "line-length"
│     └─4 text ", this preview\nstyle will format the above as:"
├─137 code "x = (\n    df\n    .filter(cond)\n    .agg(func)\n    .merge(other)\n)"
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─138 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "This deviates from the stable formatting, and also from Black, both\nof which would produce:"
├─139 code "x = (\n    df.filter(cond)\n    .agg(func)\n    .merge(other)\n)"
│       lang: "python"
│       meta: null
├─140 paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Both the stable and preview formatting are variants of something\ncalled a "
│     ├─1 strong[1]
│     │   └─0 text "fluent layout"
│     └─2 text "."
├─141 paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "In general, this preview style differs from the stable style\nonly at the first attribute that precedes\na call or subscript. The preview formatting breaks "
│     ├─1 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "before"
│     ├─2 text " this attribute,\nwhile the stable formatting breaks "
│     ├─3 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "after"
│     └─4 text " the call or subscript."
├─142 heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Sorting imports"
├─143 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "Currently, the Ruff formatter does not sort imports. In order to both sort imports and format,\ncall the Ruff linter and then the formatter:"
├─144 code "ruff check --select I --fix\nruff format"
│       lang: "shell"
│       meta: null
